berkeley-master.logic.r3: argue/equivocates: P3 (S5): "unconceived" is taken as conceiving objects while they are, in fact, not being conceived by anyone, the conceiver included (which would contradict the act of conceiving) in P3 and as conceiving objects whose represented situation is one in which no one perceives or thinks of them (what the thought is about) where conceive_unconceived is used else, a reader says: the argument holds only if they are one; taken apart, C1 (S5) does not follow (berkeley-master.logic.r3.sense1)
berkeley-master.logic.r3: argue/countermodel: C1 (S5): conceive_unconceived = T, conceive_unconceived_2 = F, conceive_without_mind = T
